hin the streets of Maipú, Antártida Argentina, San Martin, and Dr. José María Ramos Mejía.","wpu":"Plaza Canadá (Buenos Aires)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Totem_Plaza_Canada_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Torre Monumental","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.5923,"lo":-58.3737,"d":424,"ds":"Torre Monumental (Spanish for \"Monumental Tower\"), formerly known as Torre de los Ingleses (\"Tower of the English\"), is a clock tower located in the barrio (district) of Retiro in Buenos Aires, Argentina. It is situated in the Plaza Fuerza Aérea Argentina (formerly Plaza Británica) by Avenida San Martín and Avenida del Libertador.","wpu":"Torre Monumental","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/2019_Buenos_Aires_-_Torre_Monumental.jpg/960px-2019_Buenos_Aires_-_Torre_Monumental.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Torre BBVA (Buenos Aires)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.5979,"lo":-58.37013,"d":878,"ds":"The BBVA Tower (also known as the Banco Francés Tower) is an office skyscraper in Buenos Aires, Argentina, currently serving as the headquarters of BBVA Argentina financial institution. Completed between 2013 and 2016, the building stands at 155 m (509 ft) tall with 33 floors and is the eleventh tallest building in Buenos Aires.","wpu":"Torre BBVA (Buenos Aires)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/BBVA_building%2C_Buenos_Aires_%2840044125955%29.jpg/960px-BBVA_building%2C_Buenos_Aires_%2840044125955%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"AMALITA Collection","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.599444,"lo":-58.365,"d":1145,"ds":"The AMALITA Collection (Spanish: Colección AMALITA) officially the Amalia Lacroze de Fortabat Art Collection is an art museum in Buenos Aires, Argentina.","wpu":"AMALITA Collection","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Fortabat_Museum_at_Puerto_Madero.jpg/960px-Fortabat_Museum_at_Puerto_Madero.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bouchard Plaza","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.600833,"lo":-58.368889,"d":1208,"ds":"Bouchard Plaza is an architecturally significant office building in the San Nicolás ward of Buenos Aires, Argentina.","wpu":"Bouchard Plaza","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Bouchard_Plaza_desde_el_dique.JPG/960px-Bouchard_Plaza_desde_el_dique.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. Maron's Cathedral, Buenos Aires","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.59799,"lo":-58.37897,"d":1209,"ds":"The St. Maron's Cathedral (Spanish: Catedral de San Marón) also called Maronite Catholic Cathedral of Buenos Aires is a religious building of the Catholic Church (Maronite) located at 834 Paraguay Street, in the Autonomous City of Buenos Aires, Argentina.","wpu":"St. Maron's Cathedral, Buenos Aires","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Catedral_San_Mar%C3%B3n_01.jpg/960px-Catedral_San_Mar%C3%B3n_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pereda Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.5915,"lo":-58.3837,"d":1265,"ds":"The Pereda Palace is an old manor located in front of the Plazoleta Carlos Pellegrini, at the beginning of Avenida Alvear, in Buenos Aires, at number 1130 Arroyo St.","wpu":"Pereda Palace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/BrazilianEmbassyBsAs1.jpg/960px-BrazilianEmbassyBsAs1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Teatro Coliseo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.5966,"lo":-58.3833,"d":1421,"ds":"The Teatro Coliseo is a theatre in Retiro neighbourhood in Buenos Aires, Argentina which opened on July 8, 1905.","wpu":"Teatro Coliseo","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Teatro_Coliseo_%28ca._1910%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Palacio Duhau - Park Hyatt Buenos Aires","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.589167,"lo":-58.386111,"d":1477,"ds":"The Palacio Duhau - Park Hyatt Buenos Aires is a five star hotel located in the city's Recoleta section. Originally opened in 1934, the palace has been operated by multinational hospitality company Hyatt since 2006. The palace was declared national historic landmark in 2002.","wpu":"Palacio Duhau - Park Hyatt Buenos Aires","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Palacio_duhau_recoleta.jpg/960px-Palacio_duhau_recoleta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Libertad Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.603333,"lo":-58.369444,"d":1483,"ds":"The Libertad Palace, Domingo Faustino Sarmiento Cultural Center (Spanish: Palacio Libertad, Centro Cultural Domingo Faustino Sarmiento) is a cultural centre located in the Post and Telecommunications Palace in Buenos Aires, Argentina. It is the largest of Latin America, and the third or fourth largest in the world.","wpu":"Libertad Palace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Buenos_Aires_Centrum_Kirchner.jpg/960px-Buenos_Aires_Centrum_Kirchner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Italpark","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.585556,"lo":-58.386111,"d":1555,"ds":"The Italpark was a theme park in Argentina, which was located at where currently is the Parque Thays (at del Libertador and Callao), in the Recoleta neighbourhood of Buenos Aires. It became a landmark of Buenos Aires in its 30 years of existence up until its closure in 1990.","wpu":"Italpark","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Italpark.tif/lossy-page1-960px-Italpark.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Museo Mitre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.604106,"lo":-58.373969,"d":1610,"ds":"The Mitre Museum (Spanish: Museo Mitre) in Buenos Aires, Argentina, is a museum dedicated to Argentine history, as well as to the legacy of former President of Argentina, Bartolomé Mitre. The museum building is a Spanish colonial house built in 1785. It first appears in Argentine history as the refuge of the last Viceroy of Río de la Plata, Baltasar Hidalgo de Cisneros, following the May Revolution of 1810.","wpu":"Museo Mitre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Museo_Mitre.jpg/960px-Museo_Mitre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"ARA Uruguay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.604222,"lo":-58.366222,"d":1618,"ds":"The corvette ARA Uruguay, is a floating museum ship, berthed at Puerto Madero in Buenos Aires, Argentina. It was built in England and commissioned into the Argentine navy in 1874: it was decommissioned in 1926. The last of the legendary squadron of President Sarmiento, the Uruguay took part in revolutions, expeditions, rescues, and was even floating headquarters of the Navy School.","wpu":"ARA Uruguay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Corbeta_Uruguay_%28A.R.A.%29.jpg/960px-Corbeta_Uruguay_%28A.R.A.%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Parroquia de San Nicolás de Bari (Buenos Aires)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.59597,"lo":-58.38653,"d":1652,"ds":"Parroquia de San Nicolás de Bari is a Catholic church located on Santa Fe Avenue, Retiro neighborhood, Buenos Aires, Argentina.","wpu":"Parroquia de San Nicolás de Bari (Buenos Aires)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Bas%C3%ADlica_de_San_Nicol%C3%A1s_de_Bari.jpg/960px-Bas%C3%ADlica_de_San_Nicol%C3%A1s_de_Bari.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Anglican Cathedral of St. John the Baptist","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.605,"lo":-58.3714,"d":1672,"ds":"The Anglican Cathedral of St. John the Baptist is a cathedral in Buenos Aires, Argentina. It is located in Calle 25 de Mayo 282 (25 May Street) and is the oldest non-Catholic church building in Latin America.","wpu":"Anglican Cathedral of St. John the Baptist","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Catedral_San_Juan_Bautista_%28Buenos_Aires%29.JPG/960px-Catedral_San_Juan_Bautista_%28Buenos_Aires%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Teatro Gran Rex","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-34.603333,"lo":-58.378889,"d":1691,"ds":"The Teatro Gran Rex is an Art Deco style theatre in Buenos Aires, Argentina which opened on July 8, 1937, as the largest cinema in Argentina. Located near the centre of the city at 857 Corrientes Avenue, it was designed by the architect Alberto Prebisch, who was also in charge of the construction of the Obelisk, one of the main icons of the city.","wpu":"Teatro Gran Rex","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Teatro_Gran_Rex_Avenida_Corrientes.jpg/960px-Teatro_Gran_Rex_Avenida_Corrientes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Buenos Aires, officially the Autonomous City of Buenos Aires, is the capital and largest city of Argentina. It is located on the southwest of the Río de la Plata. Buenos Aires is classified as an Alpha− global city, according to the GaWC 2024 ranking.","wpu":"Buenos Aires","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Puerto_Madero%2C_Buenos_Aires_%2840689219792%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Puerto_Madero%2C_Buenos_Aires_%2840689219792%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}